<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        A comment placed ABOVE <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with the
        misleading error "unable to find matching parent xml file for CustomField". Precedent:
        Broker_Protection_Access.permissionset-meta.xml. The <description> below is a short
        summary; the rationale lives here so it never has to be trimmed to fit a cap.

        THE TOKEN CONTRACT. Three values, and which of them means DPEG depends on WHICH SIDE OF
        THE DEAL the parent LOI sits on. Getting this wrong inverts the field:

          Us     = DPEG. Correct on BOTH record types - "us" is side-neutral.
          Seller = THE COUNTERPARTY, ON BOTH SIDES. On an acquisition the counterparty really
                   is the seller. On a disposition it is not, but this is the INCUMBENT WIRE
                   TOKEN that the deployed lwc/loiCounterOffer picker already sends, so the
                   value must keep working on a sale - refusing it there would leave that Save
                   button dead against a restricted picklist.
          Buyer  = THE COUNTERPARTY, DISPOSITION ONLY. Added 2026-08-09.

        BUYER IS ADDED, NOTHING IS RENAMED. Renaming Seller -> Buyer on the disposition side
        was rejected outright: a picklist rename is a REMOVAL plus an add, which triggers the
        standing picklist-removal sweep rule (grep the repo AND query the org first) and forces
        a data migration on live acquisition rows for zero benefit. Both fields here are
        <restricted>true</restricted>, so the value has to exist in the master value set before
        any Apex can write it - four tests were failing INVALID_OR_NULL_FOR_RESTRICTED_PICKLIST
        against exactly this gap.

        BUYER IS RESTRICTED OUT OF THE Acquisition_LOI RECORD TYPE, DELIBERATELY. On a purchase
        DPEG *is* the buyer, so 'Buyer' there would name DPEG and mean the opposite of what it
        means on a sale - the same inversion this value exists to fix. Acquisition_LOI therefore
        continues to enumerate Us/Seller only; Disposition_LOI enumerates all three.
        WARNING: record-type picklist restriction is UI-ONLY and is NOT enforced by Apex DML, so
        that exclusion does not stop a direct write of 'Buyer' onto an acquisition LOI. The
        enforcement that actually holds is the record-type-aware allow-list in
        RecordStageAdvanceService plus whichever service stamps this field.

        A RECORD TYPE FILE THAT OMITS A PICKLIST SILENTLY DROPS ALL OF ITS VALUES from that
        type, so both LOI__c record types enumerate this field in full. Adding a value here
        without adding it to Disposition_LOI.recordType-meta.xml makes it invisible on the very
        record type it was added for.
    -->
    <fullName>Ball_In_Court__c</fullName>
    <description>Whose turn it is in the LOI negotiation. Us = DPEG on either side. Seller = the counterparty on either side. Buyer = the counterparty on a disposition LOI only, restricted out of Acquisition_LOI because DPEG is the buyer there. See the XML comment.</description>
    <label>Ball In Court</label>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Picklist</type>
    <valueSet>
        <restricted>true</restricted>
        <valueSetDefinition>
            <sorted>false</sorted>
            <value>
                <fullName>Us</fullName>
                <default>false</default>
                <label>Us</label>
            </value>
            <value>
                <fullName>Seller</fullName>
                <default>false</default>
                <label>Seller</label>
            </value>
            <value>
                <fullName>Buyer</fullName>
                <default>false</default>
                <label>Buyer</label>
            </value>
        </valueSetDefinition>
    </valueSet>
</CustomField>
